    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-z/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Since x = (sin θ −1)/ cos θ and y = (cos θ −1)/ sin θ, [B1] [A1] write x = tan θ −sec θ. Then x2 = tan2 θ −2 tan θ sec θ + sec2 θ = 2 tan2 θ −2 tan θ sec θ + 1 . = 2x tan θ + 1 Similarly y = cot θ −cosec θ gives y2 = 2y cot θ + 1 by cosec2 θ = 1 + cot2 θ. Thus direct squaring gives x2 −1 = 2x tan θ [M1] [A1] and y2 −1 = 2y cot θ. [A1] Multiplying gives (x2 −1)(y2 −1) = 4xy. [A1] Both coordinates lie in (−1, 0) for the stated parameter interval. [B1] (b) dx/dθ = sec2 θ −sec θ tan θ , [B1] [A1] = −x sec θ\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-z/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"and dy/dθ = −cosec2 θ + cosec θ cot θ . [M1] [A1] = y cosec θ Therefore dy/dx = −(y/x) cot θ , [M1] [A1] = (1 −y2)/(2x) using cosec θ/ sec θ = cot θ and cot θ = (y2 −1)/(2y) from part (a). [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
