      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-z/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Worked solution diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:514.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-z/q8-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) At x = 0, y = ln 4. At y = 0, 4 −2x = 1, [M1] so x = 3/2. The intercepts are (0, ln 4), (3/2, 0) . [A1] (b) T1 translates by (−4, 0); T2 stretches parallel to the x-axis by factor 1/2; T3 reflects in the y-axis. [M1] [M1] [A1] The successive equations are y = ln(x + 4), y = ln(2x + 4), y = ln(4 −2x). The resulting graph falls towards −∞as x →2−, with vertical asymptote x = 2 . [M1] [A1] (c) Rearrange ey = 4 −2x [M1] to get x = 2 −ey/2, [A1] hence f−1(x) = 2 −ex/2 . [A1] (d) The logarithm takes every real value, and its input requires 4 −2x &gt; 0, giving x &lt; 2. Interchanging the original range and domain, the inverse has domain R and range (−∞, 2) . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 8 is 12 marks.</p>"
    ],
