    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-z/q12-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The distinct acute angles θ and ϕ , θ &gt; ϕ satisfy the equation f ( θ ϕ , ) = g ( θ ϕ , ) tan ϕ , where the functions f and g are defined as f θ ϕ ( , ) ≡ sin ( θ − ϕ ) and     ( g θ ϕ , ) ≡ cos ( θ − ϕ ) − 2tan ϕ sin ( θ − ϕ ) . Use trigonometric identities to show that tan θ = 2tan ϕ . ( 10 )\" style=\"--madas-width:577.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-z/q12-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:580.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-z/q12-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Write v = tan ϕ [B1] [A1] and δ = θ −ϕ ∈(0, π/2). [M1] [A1] Rearranging gives (1 + 2v2) sin δ = v cos δ, [M1] so tan δ = v/(1 + 2v2). [A1] With u = tan θ, the subtraction identity gives tan δ = (u −v)/(1 + uv). [A1] Equating the two expressions and multiplying by the positive denominators gives (u −v)(1 + 2v2) = v(1 + uv) =⇒ u(1 + v2) = 2v(1 + v2). [M1] [A1] Expanding the two products gives u + 2uv2 −v −2v3 = v + uv2. Collecting gives u + uv2 = 2v + 2v3, and 1 + v2 &gt; 0 cancels:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-z/q12-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Thus tan θ = 2 tan ϕ. [A1] Alternative using compound angles. Write δ = θ −ϕ. In the initial equation replace tan ϕ by sin ϕ/ cos ϕ and multiply by cos2 ϕ: cos2 ϕ sin δ = sin ϕ[cos ϕ cos δ −2 sin ϕ sin δ] = sin ϕ[cos(ϕ + δ) −sin ϕ sin δ] = sin ϕ cos θ −sin2 ϕ sin δ. Collect both terms in sin δ to get (cos2 ϕ + sin2 ϕ) sin δ = sin ϕ cos θ. Expand the sine difference: sin θ cos ϕ −cos θ sin ϕ = sin ϕ cos θ, giving sin θ cos ϕ = 2 sin ϕ cos θ. Divide by cos θ cos ϕ to recover tan θ = 2 tan ϕ .\" style=\"--madas-width:477.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 12 is 10 marks.</p>"
    ],
